<Base lang="en" title="eSathi Download: How to Download Your Certificate and Documents After Login" description="How to do an eSathi download — why login is required before downloading a certificate, the Completed Applications route to the signed PDF, where the downloaded file goes, the DigiLocker and CSC options, the truth about the app, and fixes for download problems." path="/en/blog/esathi-download/" altPath="/blog/esathi-download/" faqs={faqs} updated="14 November 2026" datePublished="2026-11-14" dateModified="2026-11-14">
<div class="content-col"><h1 class="page-title">eSathi Download: The Right Way to Download Your Certificate — From Login to PDF</h1><p class="note">Note: This is an independent information guide — it is not the official portal (edistrict.up.gov.in).</p>
<p>Your certificate has been issued and the application approved — but where do you actually get the file? That is the question behind most “eSathi download” searches: how to bring the PDF of an issued certificate or document into your phone or computer. The short answer is — the download happens after login. eSathi is the citizen portal where you register, log in, apply and, at the end, download your document. This article gives that route in order: why login is required, what to check before downloading, the short route to the PDF, where the file lands, the DigiLocker and CSC options, and fixes when the download gets stuck. And if you arrived here looking for an app to download, there is a clear answer for that below too.</p>
<figure class="blog-featured"><img src="/images/blog/esathi-download-featured.webp" alt="eSathi download — getting your certificate PDF after login (example illustration)" /><figcaption>Example illustration — edistrictupseva.com</figcaption></figure>
<section class="box"><h2>Why a download needs login first</h2><p>The final signed PDF sits inside your account, under Completed Applications. It is your personal copy — so the portal first checks who you are, which is why it asks for your Login ID and password. The same arrangement means nobody else can take the file of your certificate; that is its protection. But note — not every task needs a login:</p><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Login needed?</th><th>Route</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Check application status</td><td>No</td><td>With the application/SRN number</td></tr><tr><td>Verify an issued certificate</td><td>No</td><td>With the Certificate Number or QR code</td></tr><tr><td>Download the certificate PDF</td><td>Yes</td><td>Citizen Login → Completed Applications</td></tr><tr><td>File a new application</td><td>Yes</td><td>Citizen Login</td></tr></tbody></table><div class="note">The application number (SRN) and the route to your file are two different things — the SRN only shows status; it cannot download anything. Login is the only route to the download.</div></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Before downloading — check “Certificate Generated” in the status</h2><p>There is one precondition for a download: your certificate must actually have been issued. This check needs no login:</p><ol><li>Open <strong>Application Status</strong> on the official portal.</li><li>Enter your application number (SRN) and read the status — the signed PDF exists only when it shows <strong>Certificate Generated</strong>.</li><li>If it still shows only <strong>Approved</strong>, wait for the signed PDF to be generated — the download works after that.</li></ol><p>Also keep your login details ready — the Login ID (it is not your mobile number), the password, and the registered mobile for the OTP. To understand every status, see our <a href="/en/status-verification/">Status &amp; Verification guide</a>.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>The eSathi download route — in short</h2><p>Below is the short version of the route. The detailed step-by-step for print, download and verification is in our <a href="/en/blog/certificate-print-download-verification/">Certificate Print, Download and Verification</a> article — here the focus is only on getting the PDF.</p><ol><li>Do a Citizen Login on the official eSathi portal, <strong>esathi.up.gov.in</strong>, with your Login ID and password.</li><li>On the dashboard, open the <strong>Completed Applications</strong> section.</li><li>Click your application in the list to open its full details.</li><li>Press the <strong>Print</strong> button — in the dialog that opens, choose <strong>Save as PDF</strong> instead of a printer and save the file.</li><li>Open the file and check the name spelling, date and Certificate Number — this is your official copy.</li></ol><div class="note">Prefer the portal’s own Print/download button over the browser print — the portal button delivers the correctly formatted signed document.</div></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Where the downloaded file goes + the DigiLocker option</h2><p>When you choose “Save as PDF”, the browser saves the file to the location you pick — usually your computer’s or phone’s <strong>Downloads folder</strong>. If you need the file later, look in that folder first; on a phone, the same file also appears in the browser’s download list or the Files app. As soon as you find it, copy it to a safe place under a name you will recognise.</p><h3>It may also be in DigiLocker</h3><p>Besides the portal, you can log in to <strong>DigiLocker</strong> (digilocker.gov.in) and look for your certificate under Issued Documents. A DigiLocker document is equally valid and can be shared directly with an institution — just make sure the document appears in Issued Documents from the official issuer.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Download stuck? — common problems and fixes</h2><table class="data"><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Download / Print button not visible</td><td>Check the status first — it must show Certificate Generated. Then log in and open Completed Applications; the option is there.</td></tr><tr><td>Login itself is failing</td><td>The most common cause is typing the mobile number instead of the Login ID. Full fixes for password reset, Login ID recovery and OTP are in the login article linked below.</td></tr><tr><td>PDF comes out blank</td><td>Delete the file and download again on a stable internet connection.</td></tr><tr><td>Session keeps expiring</td><td>The portal ends the session after a period of inactivity — keep all details ready beforehand, log in again and download promptly.</td></tr><tr><td>Portal not opening / server error</td><td>Use the Chrome browser, clear the cache, turn off the ad-blocker and try an incognito window. On mobile, switch on “Desktop site” mode.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The login problem is what stops the most people — its complete fix is here: <a href="/en/blog/esathi-login-forgot-password-otp/">eSathi login not working? Password and OTP fixes</a>. If the problem still continues, contact the official helpdesk — phone <strong>0522-2304706</strong> and email <strong>ceghelpdesk@gmail.com</strong>. Never share your OTP or password with anyone.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Were you looking for an “eSathi app download”?</h2><p>Many people reach here while searching an app store for an eSathi app — for them, the plain fact: <strong>no app is needed for a download.</strong> The eSathi website works on mobile just the same, and the old eSathi Android app is no longer available on the Play Store. So opening the official portal in your phone’s browser is the safe route. Do not install an app from a third-party APK file or an unknown download link — it can be dangerous for your login and your documents. If you ever install an app, take it only from a verified government publisher’s official store listing.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>If you applied through a CSC</h2><p>For CSC (Jan Seva Kendra) applicants the route is the same: create your free eSathi account and download with the method above, use DigiLocker, or collect a copy from the same centre. Having your own account makes every future task easier. If you take help at a centre, keep two things in mind — <strong>do not give your password or OTP even to the operator</strong>, and do the download with your own hands. For full details about centres, read our <a href="/en/csc-guide/">CSC guide</a>.</p></section>
<section class="box"><h2>Keep the file safe</h2><ul class="checklist"><li>Never edit the original PDF — editing breaks the digital signature and can make the certificate invalid.</li><li>Keep a copy of the original file in protected cloud storage and in an offline backup.</li><li>Never post your application number, Certificate Number or a copy of the PDF in a public group.</li><li>You may print as many copies as you like, but the original PDF is the real document — if a copy is lost, print again from that file.</li><li>On a shared device, log out when the work is done and delete identity scans left in the browser downloads.</li></ul></section>
